1. “Take your shoes off and walk barefoot if you feel bad.”
I used to laugh at this. Until one day, after a brutal conversation with my boss, I took my socks off and walked across the cold floor.
My feet burned, my breathing broke — and then, within a minute, my body genuinely switched off the alarm.
Only later did I learn that receptors in the feet are linked to the parasympathetic nervous system.
We pay for therapy, while “walk barefoot” sometimes works faster.

2. “Can’t sleep? Drink some milk.”
As a teenager, this phrase made me furious.
But at 4 a.m., after three hours of tossing and turning, I gave up. I warmed a mug — and fell asleep in 15 minutes.
Later I learned that milk contains tryptophan, a precursor to the sleep hormone.
Turns out grandma was intuitively giving what supplements are sold for today.
Some still laugh. Others quietly sleep while the first ones scroll at 5 a.m.

3. The strangest advice after a breakup: “Go peel some potatoes.”
I was furious — but after 20 minutes of cutting, the anger faded.
Hands were busy, and the brain stopped looping thoughts.
Today this is called “movement meditation.”
Grandma said it simpler: “Keep your hands busy, and your head will let go.”
We search for expensive techniques, but reality is harsher — a bowl of potatoes works faster than mantras.

4. I was crying until I was gasping — and she said, “Sing.”
I almost exploded. How do you sing when your throat is clenched?
But I tried — and within minutes, tears turned into vocal vibration, my chest softened.
Scientists call this vagus nerve stimulation.
I just felt my body take control back on its own.

5. Her final ritual: “Cover yourself with a blanket, even if you’re not cold.”
As a kid, I laughed. Later I noticed — when I wrapped myself fully in a blanket, anxiety dropped.
Today, “therapeutic weighted blankets” are sold for a lot of money, promising the same effect.
Grandma solved it with an old wool blanket.
The principle is the same: give the body pressure, and the psyche stops screaming.

So what really works better — new methods,
or simple grandmother habits that survived generations and still save people where books and coaches fail?
